In the neon haze of Neo’s terminal, the hum of the server room grew louder. In the corner sat Brendon, fingers hovering over the keyboard. The defenses of the lobby were thick wire mesh, blue-tinted eyes scanning the encoded access logs. “Pull up the session logs,” a security analyst scrolled past his view. Brendon’s monitor flickered green. “Ready?” The breach point was the 'Class-B’ vault — a multi-layered defense layer designed to slow, disrupt, and eventually eliminate unauthorized access. He didn’t run vulnerabilities in the code; he stole the account tokens.

Brendon selected a shadow profile from the network’s internal database. The firewall stood idly, software sitting idle without encryption keys. He bypassed the proxy server—a dead-end that rendered all traffic worthless. Now it only accepted the shell script he pulled from the deep web. The command was simple: brute force. Inside the shell, he fired a script that scraped every cached cookie and session key he could find. With every list mined from memory, the server screamed in protest. The biometric lock broke instantly. In one fell swoop, he dropped his password-protected credentials directly onto the console. 

The server processed the delay. An automated lockout kicked in immediately. He watched the dashboard revoke his account, declaring the termination of the attacker in two seconds flat. It was not magic;
